<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luapula River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Luapula</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Luapula is a river of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, part of the upper Congo system, that flows from the Bangweulu swamps north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mweru">Lake Mweru</a> and forms much of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Its fertile, fish-rich valley was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazembe">Kazembe</a> from about 1740.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luapula-river.jpg" alt="Luapula River" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kwamikagami · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luvua_-_Luapula_-_Chambeshi_River_DRC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
